  "image_name": "0f8ef20965f1ecd075af648457e703c7.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes several logic-gated CAR T-cell designs that aim to improve tumour specificity and safety. A central CAR T cell is surrounded by target cells carrying different surface markers, and the colored receptor cartoons indicate modular engineered receptors with antigen-binding scFvs, transmembrane regions, signalling domains, inhibitory modules, and transcriptional regulators.\n\nAcross the figure, the main idea is that T-cell activation can be restricted to tumour contexts by requiring combinations of cues rather than a single antigen. One strategy appears to be dual-antigen recognition, in which two tumour antigens on the same target cell cooperate to trigger activation. Another uses tumour microenvironment signals such as low pH or hypoxia to condition receptor function. A further design shows protease-sensitive masking, where tumour-associated proteases cleave a blocking region to expose the receptor only near tumours. There is also an inhibitory “NOT” logic concept in which recognition of a non-malignant tissue marker suppresses activation, helping spare healthy cells. Finally, one module seems to use a synNotch-like mechanism: antigen engagement causes cleavage and translocation of a transcription factor that then induces CAR expression.\n\nOverall, the figure compares complementary engineering approaches for making CAR T cells respond selectively in cancer while reducing off-tumour effects.",
